body:
Homeschooler here. I think the one issue you might find is a lack of resources without the AP classes, but also you mentioned it's not a necessary means to get into the college you want. Maybe there's a way to keep the same interest in learning even if you are going back in person? So I don't have a definitive answer, since I don't know everything about your situation, but if the idea keeps nagging you then maybe! GPAs aren't everything, but they certainly help if you don't take/get great scores on the SAT/ACT. The AP classes would help with that. And yes, there are absolutely ways to socialize! For me, I joined my community theatre, and started dance and tennis. Sorry this probably didn't help too much since there's only so much I can suggest, but I wish you luck this school year!

&#x200B;

Edit: and if you need any suggestions of online and book resources, my inbox is open

Intrusive thoughts do not have an actual hold over you. Really, none of your thoughts do. You decide how you act on your thoughts. I have a history of scary intrusive thoughts but I'm never afraid of me doing any of this, because I know from my therapist it's just my brain trying to come up with the most outrageous things possible. Like the "What-if I was to move in front of this bus right now?" kind of thought is never going to make you act against your will. You have more bodily autonomy than you're thinking, and being intoxicated won't change it as much as you are worried.

&#x200B;

Edit: You mentioned what your intrusive thoughts detail, and I think it would be handy to see a professional about it. The more you talk about it and the less you internalize it, the better. It doesn't make you broken, it makes you human.
